"facts": [
"The JLPT (Japanese-Language Proficiency Test) has 5 levels: N5 is the easiest and N1 the hardest. N5 is the first step.",
"The JLPT is held twice a year, in July and December, and Indian cities such as Bengaluru, Chennai, Mumbai and New Delhi are test centres.",
"Japanese and Kannada put words in the same order: subject, object, verb. 'I water drink' - watashi wa mizu o nomimasu - ನಾನು ನೀರು ಕುಡಿಯುತ್ತೇನೆ.",
"Japanese has the same five vowel sounds as Kannada: a, i, u, e, o - like ಅ ಇ ಉ ಎ ಒ. That makes pronunciation easy for us.",
"Japanese adds small words after a noun (wa, o, ni), just as Kannada adds endings like -ಅನ್ನು and -ಗೆ.",
"Japanese is written with three scripts: Hiragana, Katakana and Kanji. You start with Hiragana.",
"Hiragana has 46 basic letters. Two a day means you know them all in about 3 weeks.",
"Katakana is used for foreign words: インド (indo) is India, and バス (basu) is bus.",
"Kanji came from Chinese characters. For JLPT N5 you need about 100 of them.",
"Japan's government gives the MEXT scholarship to foreign students, including from India: it pays tuition, a monthly allowance and the air ticket.",
"Indian students apply for the MEXT scholarship through the Embassy of Japan in India.",
"Japan has a work visa called Specified Skilled Worker for jobs such as nursing care, hotels, food service and construction.",
"For the Specified Skilled Worker visa you show Japanese at about JLPT N4 level plus a skills test - N5 is the step before N4.",
"India and Japan signed an agreement in January 2021 to help Indian workers go to Japan under the Specified Skilled Worker programme.",
"Japan's population is getting older and many fields need young workers - one reason Japan welcomes skilled people from India.",
"Many Japanese companies work in India - Toyota, Honda, Suzuki, Sony, Panasonic and Hitachi - and they value staff who can talk with Japanese teams.",
"A JLPT certificate does not expire - pass N5 once and it stays on your résumé for life.",
"The JLPT tests three things: vocabulary, grammar with reading, and listening.",
"Like German A1 or French A1, JLPT N5 is the starting level of a new language - and it is something extra on your résumé.",
"About 125 million people speak Japanese, and Japan is one of the world's largest economies.",
"In Japan, people add -san after a name to show respect - like we say -ಅವರು or -ji.",
"Japan's money is the yen. Tokyo is the capital.",
"Japanese trains are famous for being on time - punctuality matters a lot at work in Japan.",
"Saying 'hajimemashite' and bowing slightly is how people introduce themselves in Japan - a good first line in an interview.",
"Tip: 10 minutes of Japanese every day beats 2 hours once a week. Little and often.",
"Tip: say every word out loud. Speaking helps you remember it much faster than only reading.",
"Tip: learn numbers early - ichi, ni, san - you use them for prices, time and phone numbers.",
"Tip: Japanese questions end with 'ka' - 'genki desu ka?' means 'Are you fine?'",
"Tip: 'desu' at the end of a sentence makes it polite - 'gakusei desu' means 'I am a student'.",
"Tip: the Japanese track in your 🎯 Placement page teaches the letters first, step by step - 5 minutes a day is enough."
